# False positives reproduced independently of application code with prebuilt
# conda-forge PDAL 2.10.2, PROJ 9.9.0, and Qt 6.12.0 on Linux ARM64
# (Clang TSan 22.1.8).
# Revisit these exclusions when upgrading or instrumenting those dependencies.
# https://github.com/google/sanitizers/wiki/ThreadSanitizerCppManual#non-instrumented-code
# https://github.com/google/sanitizers/wiki/ThreadSanitizerSuppressions

# LAS previews share a function-local static const dimension table. TSan sees
# its allocation and later copies but misses the prebuilt library's atomic
# initialization guard. Initializing before worker creation removes the report.
# https://github.com/PDAL/PDAL/blob/2.10.2/io/private/las/Utils.cpp
race:pdal::las::pdrfDims

# Parsing equivalent WKT parameter names initializes a function-local static
# const set. Concurrent parsing with independent PROJ contexts reproduces the
# report; initialization before creating workers removes it. TSan misses the
# prebuilt library's initialization guard, just as with PDAL's table above.
# https://github.com/OSGeo/PROJ/blob/9.9.0/src/iso19111/operation/oputils.cpp
race:osgeo::proj::operation::areEquivalentParameters

# Qt protects its event queue with atomic/futex synchronization that TSan
# cannot see in this prebuilt library. Concurrent postEvent calls reproduce
# reports in QPostEventList::addEvent and QArrayData::reallocateUnaligned.
# Some stacks omit addEvent, so a function-specific rule is insufficient.
# Exclude intercepted accesses originating in Linux QtCore only; application
# code stays instrumented. This also reduces detection inside QtCore itself.
# https://github.com/qt/qtbase/blob/v6.12.0/src/corelib/kernel/qcoreapplication.cpp
called_from_lib:libQt6Core.so
